<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Gao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">351–417</span> · Also known as Duke Wuzhao of Western Liang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a> was a Chinese official of the Longxi Li family who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> under Northern Liang and in 400 declared himself duke, founding Western Liang, the westernmost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a>. He moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiuquan">Jiuquan</a> in 405 and kept Chinese learning alive on the far frontier. The Tang emperors later claimed him as their ancestor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-gao.jpg" alt="Li Gao" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1917年修《安徽黟县鹤山李氏宗谱》 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%9D%8E%E6%9A%A0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
